+# Philosophy
+
+For whoever proposes a change to how larder is built, such as a new service, store or dependency, before writing the
+proposal. It states what the volunteers who run larder favor when options conflict. Written after issue 70, a
+proposal to add Elasticsearch for pantry item search, made the team argue out whether to run one more service for the
+third time, after Redis for sessions in 2023 and Redis for the job queue in 2025, each time from scratch.
+
+## Tenets
+
+- **A new service has to do something Postgres cannot, and two volunteers must be able to restore it.** Larder runs
+  one Postgres database and one web app, kept by volunteers who are not always around; a service only one of them can
+  restore is down until that person is back. In March 2025 the Redis instance holding the job queue evicted 300
+  receipt jobs on a Saturday night, and only one volunteer could restore it.
+  - Rules out: a service added for a job Postgres already does, such as Redis for sessions or for a job queue, or
+    Elasticsearch for item search, where Postgres full-text search on the item name brought a search for "rice" from
+    four seconds to 40 ms and found "Rice, brown". It rules out too a service only one volunteer knows how to restore.
+  - Does not rule out: a service someone else runs. The card gateway is outside larder, and a hosted service nobody
+    on the team has to run would be accepted.
+  - Applied in: [keep sessions in the database](adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md) and
+    [run background jobs on Postgres](adrs/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
